        "section": "methods",
        "text": "and other constituents to adjust the water quality conditions as appro - priate. The major water quality conditions examined include pH, ionic strength, and NOM concentration, while the operation parameters that have been tested include air flow rate, addition of a thickener, and addition of Fe (III). A stratified experiment design was adopted in which one parameter was varied while the others were kept constant, and the conditions tested are tabulated in Table S1 in the supporting information (SI). Each set of FF experiment was operated by supplying air flow for 2 min, during which all the foam formed on the water column surface was collected to the receiving flask through the funnel. The duration of 2 min Y. Wang et al. Chemosphere 311 (2023) 137004 3 was used because no effective foam formation was observed after 2 min of air supply in preliminary tests. The funnel, tubing, and the flask were rinsed with 10 mL methanol after FF operation. The rinsate was com - bined with the collected foam, during which process the foam collapsed to a solution. The volume of the foam solution was then measured before 0.5-mL duplicate samples were taken from both the bottom solution and the collected foam solution. A multi-stage FF treatment experiment, schematically represented in Fig."
      },
      {
        "locator_id": "S01-E02",
        "corpus_chunk": 37,
        "section": "methods",
        "text": "Y. Wang et al. Chemosphere 311 (2023) 137004 9 much less effectively than PFHxS (83% vs. 97%, overall) The enrichment of PFASs in the foam generally increased along with the chain length within PFCAs and PFSAs (Fig. 3c), consistent with an earlier study on PFAS removal by FF (Ebersbach et al., 2016). This is due to higher interfacial partitioning of PFASs with longer chain length (Goss et al., 2006; Campbell et al., 2009). The overall recovery for each of the PFASs after the multi-stage FF treatment was within 70\u2013130%, except for PFDA (Fig. S11). In order to further explore mass balance of AFFF-contaminated groundwater during FF treatment, including the effect of adding HPMC, an additional single- stage experiment was conducted under the same conditions. The average TOC recovery was 111.3% (Fig. S9). PFASs accounted for <0.05% of TOC in samples (Table S6), whereas HPMC contributed 93.9% of the TOC in the pre-treated original solution (Fig. S12). This suggests that most HPMC was removed from the treated bottom solution and collected in the foam after the treatment, since the TOC from HPMC"
      },
      {
        "locator_id": "S01-E03",
        "corpus_chunk": 8,
        "section": "methods",
        "text": "2.3.1. Setup The FF treatment experiments were performed in batch mode. The FF setup is schematically represented in Fig. 1 a. The column is made of acrylic plastic, with the inner diameter of 5 cm and height of 30 cm. An air stone (5-cm diameter, 10-cm height) was installed at the bottom of the column. Air was introduced through an orifice on the wall to the air stone by an adjustable air pump at a prescribed air flow rate, with the rate monitored by a gas flow meter. The foam was collected through a polypropylene funnel to a 1000-mL flask (Nalgene\u2122, Rochester, NY, USA) through gum rubber tubing (Cole-Parmer\u2122, Vernon Hills, IL, USA) using a vacuum pump (Welch\u2019s\u2122, Concord, MA, USA). 2.3.2. Experiments FF treatment experiments were first conducted with laboratory- spiked PFAS solution to explore the efficiency of FF treatment in rela - tion to key water quality conditions and major operation conditions. Each treatment involves 200 mL of a solution prepared with deionized (DI) water containing 100 \u03bc g/L each of PFBA, PFOA, 6:2 FTS, and PFOS, and other constituents to adjust the water quality conditions as appro - priate."
      },
      {
        "locator_id": "S01-E04",
        "corpus_chunk": 38,
        "section": "results",
        "text": "suggests that most HPMC was removed from the treated bottom solution and collected in the foam after the treatment, since the TOC from HPMC in the collected foam accounted for 87.2% of total TOC (Fig. S12). The near complete removal of HPMC further supports its promising usage as a safe additive in FF treatment, although FDA and EU already authorized its use in food (Burdock, 2007). TOP assay results for each terminal PFAA in the original sample, treated bottom solution, and foam solution from the single-stage treatment of AFFF-contaminated groundwater are shown in Fig. S13. Five PFCAs exhibited concentration increases after the oxidative treatment, including PFBA, PFPeA, PFHxA, PFHpA, PFOA, and PFUdA, while the only PFSA with higher concentration afterwards was PFOS (Figs. S13a, S13b, and S13c). This agrees with the finding by other studies about PFAA precursor present in AFFF formulations and AFFF-impacted environmental samples (Houtz et al., 2013; Houtz et al., 2016; Schaefer et al., 2018). The recovery of each terminal PFAA was within 70%\u2013130% (Fig. S13d). The mass balance in samples of AFFF-contaminated water was thus deemed satisfactory regarding the recoveries of target PFASs, TOC, and the terminal PFAAs in the TOP assay."
      },
      {
        "locator_id": "S01-E05",
        "corpus_chunk": 5,
        "section": "results",
        "text": "(Horst, 2020). Although there have been several studies on removing PFASs from water via FF at bench scale (Meng et al., 2018; Robey, 2020; McCleaf et al., 2021; Smith et al., 2022) and full scale (Burns et al., 2021; Burns et al., 2022), more studies are needed to explore the effect of water qualities and operation conditions on the performance of this promising technology. Efforts have been made to assess certain thick - eners, alkyl glucose amide, xanthan gum, and sodium carboxymethyl - cellulose, to facilitate foaming during FF treatment, but was not completely successful ( He et al., 2019). Although xanthan gum could retard the drainage of foam, addition of alkyl glucose amide and sodium carboxymethylcellulose was found counteracting foam expansion when used as foam-facilitators in AFFF. In this study, experiments were first carried out on laboratory-spiked water samples to explore the optimal operation conditions of FF treat - ment for PFAS removal in water of different properties. The conditions were then applied to experiments conducted on different environmental water samples containing PFASs, including a landfill leachate, an AFFF- contaminated groundwater, and a wastewater treatment plant (WWTP) effluent sample. In addition to targeted PFASs analyzed in these tests,"
      },
      {
        "locator_id": "S01-E06",
        "corpus_chunk": 17,
        "section": "results",
        "text": "3. Results and discussion 3.1. FF performance in relation to operation and water quality conditions Experiments were first conducted with laboratory-spiked water samples to explore optimal operation conditions of FF treatment for PFAS removal in water of different water quality conditions. A stratified experimental design was adopted in which one parameter was varied while the other kept constant, with the conditions tested listed in Table S1. 3.1.1. Effect of air flow rate A set of experiments were performed with the air flow rate adjusted to 4, 7, 10, and 15 L/min (Column \u2460, Table S1), and the results are shown in Fig. S2. It shows that the PFAS removal efficiency increased as the air flow rate increased (Fig. S2a). The highest removal efficiency for PFBA, PFOA, and 6:2 FTS was achieved at 31%, 66%, and 62%, respectively, at the air flow rate 15 L/min, while that of PFOS reached 88% at the air flow rate 10 L/min. The enrichment factor of PFAS however decreased as the air flow rate increased, because the greater flow rate led to higher volume of the foam solution. To better under - stand this phenomenon at the mechanistic level, the FF process can be"
      },
